Subject: NFS packet size (was: compiling 2.1.9x + MILO for LX)
Date: 1998/03/31
Message-ID: <84emzi6pw8.fsf_-_@sweat.cs.unm.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 339748475
References: <87yaxu8rna.fsf@snot.cs.unm.edu> <6fk5t1$lqc@news3.newsguy.com> <87ra3m8bch.fsf@snot.cs.unm.edu>
Organization: University of New Mexico, Albuquerque
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


To follow up my own post ... a little benchmarking showed that 8k nfs
packets give much faster NFS read times, on lightly loaded 10 and 100
megabit ethernet networks, between two Linux 2.0.33 machines.  The
advantage is more pronounced for 100.  This appears to be in
contradiction to some information posted by Donald Becker, so
doubtless some details of my setup did not match his.  It would be
interesting to know the cause of the inefficiency with small rsize.

Here are the figures I got for transfering a 16megabyte file
(consisting of repeated copies of /boot/vmlinuz up to the appropriate
file size) over 100baseT, from a Pentium Pro 200MHz server to a Alpha
LX 533MHz client.  40 trials at each packet size.  The script I used
is included below.

	rsize time (secs, +/- stdev)
	----- ---- 
	1024  19.4 +/- 1.8
	2048  12.8 +/- 1.6
	4096   8.5 +/- 1.4
	8192   3.5 +/- 0.4
	16384  3.3 +/- 0.4

----------------------------------------------------------------

#! /bin/sh

# "test-nfs" script.  Grep and awk and gnuplot the output.

case `hostname --short` in
 faceplant)
	server=sweat
	mdir=/var
	cdir=/nfs/sweat/var
	sdir=tmp
	fname=foo
	fname2=bar
	remuid=bap
	;;
 clipping)
	cdir=/nfs/sweat			# local mount point
	server=sweat			# server host
	mdir=/				# directory on server to be mounted
	sdir=usr/tmp			# directory on server where test file lives
	fname=foo			# name of test file
	fname2=bar			# name of place to copy test file to
	remuid=bap			# remote user id
	;;
 *)
	echo "unprogrammed host"
	exit 1
esac

for t in 1 2 3 4 5 ; do
  for buf in 32768 16384 8192 4096 2048 1024 ; do	# mount buffer size

    umount $cdir
    mount -o rsize=$buf,wsize=$buf,ro,nosuid $server:$mdir $cdir

    for i in 1 2 3 4 ; do

      #ssh -l bap -a -x sweat rm /$sdir/$fname
      #ssh -l bap -a -x sweat dd if=/dev/zero of=/$sdir/$fname bs=8196 count=2048
      ssh -l $remuid -a -x $server "cd $mdir/$sdir; cp $fname $fname2; mv $fname2 $fname"

      echo NFS packet size: $buf
      date
      time cat < $cdir/$sdir/$fname | wc -c > /dev/null
      date
    done
  done
done



