In article <35066076.41C6@adonis39.et.deakin.edu.au>,
Weiping Hu  <whu@adonis39.et.deakin.edu.au> wrote:
>I have one hard disk, which is partioned into a, b, c, d, e, f, g under
>Digital Unix.  At the moment the partition g is empty with UFS file
>system.  When I booted up Linux, it couldn't see this partition, so I
>couldn't install it.  Linux did report seeing the hard disk, but without
>the partition table.  Since there is a large amount of information on
>the other partitions, I dared not to run the fdisk program.

I don't think Linux even recognizes the DEC partioning scheme. For some
strange reason, it is stuck with the ridiculous, archaic and inflexible
partitioning scheme from DOS. They'd have done better for the Alpha
to either use the "native" (DEC) partitioning scheme, or use the Apple
partitioning scheme.

Well, ok, there's one reason to have used it, and that's if you want to
dual-boot with NT which, of course, also uses the DOS partitioning scheme.

Good thing you didn't use fdisk; writing back the partition table would
have trashed your current map.



