: >I have one hard disk, which is partioned into a, b, c, d, e, f, g under
: >Digital Unix. 
:
: I don't think Linux even recognizes the DEC partioning scheme.

Yes, it does.  Only you have to boot a proper kernel from SRM console
with a help of 'aboot' loader.

: For some
: strange reason, it is stuck with the ridiculous, archaic and inflexible
: partitioning scheme from DOS.

This "strange reason" is called compatitibility.  If you are booting
using ARC/AlphaBIOS console, which knows anything only about this
"ridiculous, archaic" and/or you want it to coexist with NT - which
do not know anything else, then you are stuck with this partitioning.

Not that equally archaic BSD partitioning scheme is so much better
on disks of sizes as they are coming nowadays.  But Linux can live
with both.  Check what 'minlabel', which you will find among your
executables, does.

  Michal




