On Wed, 04 Mar 1998 17:15:22 -0500, Trevor Zion Bauknight <trev@sc.edu> wrote:
>I was checking out the DEC website for some info about some used equipment
>I'm looking at (VAX) and I was wondering why so many Alpha fans are bent
>on NT or Linux instead of OpenVMS.  It looks like a heck of a fine OS in
>many ways...is it just extremely expensive, DiffiCuLt to use?  What?

Licensing costs have traditionally been substantially higher, which is
a problem.

You don't get compatibility with any great body of either UNIX-based
software (when running Linux) or the body of Windows software (when
running NT). 

You'll find some VMS fans out there, and probably don't have to look
*TOO* hard.

But there's a highly common perception that VMS is an obsolete system.
A lot of VARs certainly consider this to be the case; there's not a lot
of new software being ported to OpenVMS.  

Digital themselves are almost falling over themselves trying to provide
tools to port VMS software over to NT.  Their heavy "partnership" with
Microsoft struck me as more of an organ donation than anything else,
judging by the effects of most past "partnerships" between vendors and
MS. 

Note that I haven't said anything thus far that could be used to
conclude *anything* technically about the relative merits of
VMS/NT/Linux...  Very little about the increasing levels of disinterest
in VMS has anything to do with technology... 

-- 
