Good questions.  I spent 3.5 years in the VMS world on both Vax and Alpha. 
The VMS architecture is quite stable and good (it was developed by Dave
Culter who Microsoft borrowed to write NT 3.1).  The main drawback that I
saw was that VMS is proprietary to Digital.  Installing software, services,
etc in VMS requires not only the distribution, but also one or more
licenses from DEC that are usually expensive (until about 2 years ago it
cost over $5000 to put DECs TCP/IP on VMS).  It also seems that development
in VMS is destined (like OS/2, etc.) to end up in a purely maintenance mode
(with little if any further advancements).  It is easy to use (very wordy
command structure) and robust, but I think that its future is questionable.

