:> codes which showed poor performance were for computational neuroscience.
:
:Sure, in many fields, elementary functions are critical.
:
:It would be good if someone would be inspired to go fix that library.

Yes it would be.  However, looking at the source it's not obvious to me what
exactly needs to be done to "fix" it.

:> And why does the Linux/Alpha compiler compile the "FDLIBM" library (a high
:> quality IEEE-754 libm) so poorly?  
:
:Is this library fast on other platforms? It might pay to hand-optimize the
:output; it's often hard for compilers to compile weird shifts efficiently.

I don't know what counts as ''fast'', but I compiled FDLIBM from identical
sources on x86 and Alpha with egcs 1.0.1 on Linux/Alpha , and tested a
benchmark computational neuronal network program written in C.  The compiler
flags were -O3 -ffast-math and on x86 -malign-double, and linked with my
personally compiled libm and not the system one.

This program exercises only double precision 'exp' heavily, being about 40%
of the runtime.

On a 300 Mhz Pentium II the total runtime was 15.07 seconds, and on a 533
LX164 it was 13.27.

On trigonometric functions (tested in other programs), the Alpha seems to be
at a slightly greater disadvantage.

