Arun Sharma <asharma@netscape.com> writes:

> The following program compiles on Intel but fails to compile on
> Alpha. I've tried both gcc-2.7.2.3 and egcs-1.0.1.
> 
> Can someone tell me why ?
> 
> 	-Arun
> 
> extern int f(char);
> 
> typedef struct foo1 {
> 	int i;
> 	char c;
> } f1;
> 
> 
> typedef struct foo2 {
> 	int (*foo_func)(char);
> 	int j;
> } f2;
> 
> typedef struct foo {
> 	f1 F1;
> 	f2 F2;
> } mytype;
> 
> mytype foo1 = {
> 	{ 10, 'c'},
> 	{(int) f, 20}
> };

Remove the cast to "int" and it will compile.  On a 64-bit platforms
such as Linux/Alpha, a pointer does not fit into an int, hence the
error message.  If the source of your real problem is initialization
of a union, there are several options to fix the problem (reorder the
members in the union, depend on a gcc-specific extension to initialize
unions, or use longs instead of ints).

	--david



