From: edhall@ayched.com (Ed Hall)
Subject: Re: gcc  Alpha/Intel incompatibility
Date: 1998/03/07
Message-ID: <EpFpKu.MEF@ayched.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 331632848
References: <sn0k9a7xq7z.fsf@zugzwang.mcom.com>
Followup-To: comp.os.linux.development.system,comp.os.linux.alpha
Organization: H-Ed Software
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.development.system,comp.os.linux.alpha


Arun Sharma (asharma@netscape.com) wrote:

: The following program compiles on Intel but fails to compile on
: Alpha. I've tried both gcc-2.7.2.3 and egcs-1.0.1.

: Can someone tell me why ?

It's invalid code.  You cast the pointer-to-function initializer
to an "int":

    mytype foo1 = {
        { 10, 'c'},
        {(int) f, 20}
    };

Change this to:

    mytype foo1 = {
        { 10, 'c'},
        { f, 20}
    };

and it compiles fine.

On an Alpha, it is impossible to represent a function pointer (a
64-bit quantity) with an int (a 32-bit quantity).  Thus the
initializer fails, whereas on Intel where sizeof(pointer) ==
sizeof(int) you only get a warning.  You DID see the warning,
didn't you?

Admittedly, the error is cryptic, even misleading.  In that
sense, it's a bug.  But GCC is hardly alone in sometimes
getting confused when faced with invalid programs...

                -Ed Hall
                edhall@ayched.com



