g++ 2.7 and  egcs 1.0.1 running on  a linux-alpha-glibc
system
is not taking kindly to small double values - those funky
near zero values that you know should be zero, but have
stupid values
like 2.38723e-314.

The problem does not show itself on my linux-intel-glibc
system. 


#include <iostream.h>
#include <cmath>

int main()
{
        cerr << "hello" << endl;
        
        double u = 2.38723e-314;

        cerr  << "u = " << u << " M_PI = " << M_PI << endl;
        cerr << "u * M_PI = " << u * M_PI << endl;

        return 0;
}

The results:

on linux - alpha - glibc
+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

[cliff@van cliff]$ g++ hello.c++ -g -o hello   <-- using gcc
2.7.1 
[cliff@van cliff]$ ./hello
hello
u = Floating point exception (core dumped)
[cliff@van cliff]$ eg++ hello.c++ -g -o hello   <-- using
egcs 1.0.1 
[cliff@van cliff]$ ./hello
hello
u = 2.38723e-314 M_PI = 3.14159
u * M_PI = Floating point exception (core dumped)


slightly different results, but both hosed. 



on linux - intel - glibc 
+++++++++++++++++++++++++

[cliff@brother_brent cliff]$ eg++ hello.c++ -g -o hello
[cliff@brother_brent cliff]$ hello
hello
u = 2.38723e-314 M_PI = 3.14159
u * M_PI = 7.4997e-314

No problem ( with either compiler ) . 

Can anybody explain this? Is it a known problem?  Can it be
fixed. Must I
resort to float's?

Thanks for any help.

cliff



