> on linux - intel - glibc
> +++++++++++++++++++++++++
> 
> [cliff@brother_brent cliff]$ eg++ hello.c++ -g -o hello
> [cliff@brother_brent cliff]$ hello
> hello
> u = 2.38723e-314 M_PI = 3.14159
> u * M_PI = 7.4997e-314
> 
> No problem ( with either compiler ) .
> 
> Can anybody explain this? Is it a known problem?  Can it be
> fixed. Must I
> resort to float's?

On an x86, 2.38723e-314 is a valid representation as a denormal,
and can be loaded onto the processor as such.  The alpha
does not handle denormals in the processor, and generates
an exception when they are encountered.  You can't represent
2.38723e-314 as a normal IEEE double precision number.



