People often post to this newsgroup asking about UDB (aka DEC Multia)
performance.  I ran Bytemark on one, and here are the results.  They
should interest those who are considering buying one of these
machines or who are interested in Linux/Alpha performance.

The machine has: 233MHz 21066A CPU, 512K L2 cache, 24MB ram.  The
compiler is gcc 2.8.0, and the libraries are glibc-1.99 (the default
supplied with RedHat 4.2).  

/usr/local/gcc/bin/gcc -Wall -mieee -O2 -funroll-loops \
-finline-functions -fomit-frame-pointer -o nbench *.c -lm
Mon Mar  9 19:47:47 PST 1998

BBBBBB   YYY   Y  TTTTTTT  EEEEEEE
BBB   B  YYY   Y    TTT    EEE
BBB   B  YYY   Y    TTT    EEE
BBBBBB    YYY Y     TTT    EEEEEEE
BBB   B    YYY      TTT    EEE
BBB   B    YYY      TTT    EEE
BBBBBB     YYY      TTT    EEEEEEE

BYTEmark (tm) Native Mode Benchmark ver. 2 (3/95)
NUMERIC SORT:  Iterations/sec.: 47.953229  Index: 1.239262
STRING SORT:  Iterations/sec.: 2.952712  Index: 1.297895
BITFIELD:  Iterations/sec.: 14078497.703146  Index: 2.414908
FP EMULATION:  Iterations/sec.: 4.517243  Index: 2.171751
FOURIER:  Iterations/sec.: 424.228038  Index: 0.480352
ASSIGNMENT:  Iterations/sec.: 0.658034  Index: 2.507084
IDEA:  Iterations/sec.: 142.039861  Index: 2.173192
HUFFMAN:  Iterations/sec.: 64.970504  Index: 1.805489
NEURAL NET:  Iterations/sec.: 0.266237  Index: 0.450485
LU DECOMPOSITION:  Iterations/sec.: 15.424358  Index: 0.910636
...done...
===========OVERALL============
INTEGER INDEX: 1.879918
FLOATING-POINT INDEX: 0.581921
 (90 MHz Dell Pentium = 1.00)
==============================

This benchmark is bit old, but the numbers seem reasonable.  Integer
results look a bit high, but floating point seems about right.  It
would be interesting to see the results with a 166 MHz/256K machine,
as those are the most common.

The Bytemarks can be found on www.byte.com.  Some small modifications
are necessary to the header files to get them to compile; mail me if
you are interested.  The -mieee flag was necessary to avoid an FPE.







