Chris Saunderson <Chris.Saunderson@adelaide.maptek.com.au> writes:


> "Pleae insert your boot disk in your first drive if it's not there
> already".
> 
> Ok, so I throw in my MILO disk, or my Kernel disk or even my ramdisk,
> or even a disk created which has the kernel I used on it, and
> it pukes and tells me there was an invalid argument to mount.
> 
> 
> Huh? Can anyone shed light on this?


Well, as it turns out, I shed my own light on it.


The final step in the installation process requires you to have a
ext2 formatted floppy with a file called "vmlinux.gz" on it. If you
don't have that, then the installation process will quite happily
sit there until it _does_ have a file called vmlinux.gz to copy.


So, 2 problems found and solved. If you've got an AlphaStation 200 4/233,
use the avanti-s image, unless it's got a ZLxp in it - then you'll
need to use the avanti image (not the XLT as the doco indicates). 
Number 2: make sure you've got a floppy with your avanti image copied
to vmlinux.gz in it for the installation :)


