On 27 Feb 1998 01:16:19 GMT, Greg Lindahl <lindahl@pbm.com> wrote:
:mbkennel@yahoo.com (Matt Kennel) writes:
:
:> I find that the floating performance of gcc/egcs is even worse on
:> Linux/Alpha.   With egcs I get about 2x a P2 300 on integer codes, and
:> typically no more than equal on FP codes and sometimes less.  The 'libm' is
:> a big problem, exponentials and trigonometric functions are very poor
:> performing on Linux/Alpha, even when recompiling Netlib's fdlibm with
:> egcs -O3 blah blah blah.
:
:Thank you for finally getting around to explaining what your code
:does. 

I tried a variety of different programs.  Apparently the standard functions
are so bad that they became the bottleneck in all of them.  I profiled a
couple of them.

I don't know what SPECfp programs do, but it's impossible to do optical
physics without exponentials, sine and cosine (over both R and C).

:That limitation is well known and no one has volunteered to attempt
:to fix the linux libm.

It's remarkable that it's so poor---apparently the functions are so much
worse than on x86 that it can negate all the advantage of a supposedly 2.5x
SPECmark advantage. 

What I don't understand is why it should be so.  It superficially appears
from the symbols that the standard Linux/Alpha and Linux/x86 libm is either
identical to, or closely derived from, Netlib's "fdlibm". It also appears to
be the standard 'libm' for Sparcs, and was originally written by somebody at
Sun.

What's so magically bad about 'fdlibm'? 

Looking at the source for "fdlibm" I don't see anything obvious which would
make it particularly bad---all the things which fdlibm routines do seem to
be ones which some "typical" numerical programs would do all the time, if
you take away the bit masking and exponent shifting.

Is that it? 

At the moment it would be an unfair accusation, but I'll admit that I'm
worried Alpha is a great Specmark chip and not really a good science chip.

:> Will you please port DEC C + Fortran + DEC libm to the Alpha under linux?
:
:If you want to join the list of interested parties, email me how many
:licenses for which platform you'd be intersted (x86, alpha, c/c++ and
:fortran.)

At the moment, 1 Fortran 90 for Linux/Alpha.  If it works well soon, we will
probably continue to buy Alphas for computation---otherwise we stick with
x86 Linux, or with lower probability, try R10000's and Ultrasparcs.  I don't
know about the new Sparcs, but in a brief experience with a R10000 and
Sgi/Cray F90 I was quite impressed by the real life performance.

We'd like to limit our system administration burden (hence Linux/Alpha to
add to our Linux/x86), because it basically comes down to me and I'd rather
study ''chaos theory'' than computers in chaos.

