I run recently a few timing runs on a totally untouched Fortran code
supplied by a prospective customer.  It does some kind of a transform
using a grid (looks like a kernel for ODE solving) and is chockfull of
sin() and cos() calls in matrix type calculations.  I am pretty sure
that a day with an editor would speed up this code quite a bit.

Anyway, when using 1024x1024 grid I got times a bit above 3 minutes for a
code compiled with just -O3, g77 from egcs-1.0.1 and running on 533 LX.
Adding into a fray things like '-funroll-loops -fomit-frame-pointer
-fstrength-reduce' dropped this time down to 2 minutes with small seconds.
Not a bad result for a short edit in a Makefile. :-)  Curiously enough
putting in also '-ffast-math' made this test not very much but
noticeably and consistently **slower**.

This customer runs this code right now on 500au DEC machine using
Digital Unix and its compiler.  Their times are between four and five
minutes for the same test, I understand.  Granted, this is not the
same hardware and probably using DU and its Fortran on LX would
drop times even further but I think that LX with Linux is not a slouch
for the money.  And, of course, YOU can improve it even further.

  Michal




