mbkennel@yahoo.com (Matt Kennel) wrote:

[ I thought of answering this one instead of the reply to my own question
  about your code (much more productive :-) ]

> I tried a variety of different programs.  Apparently the standard functions
> are so bad that they became the bottleneck in all of them.  I profiled a
> couple of them.
> 
> I don't know what SPECfp programs do, but it's impossible to do optical
> physics without exponentials, sine and cosine (over both R and C).

You bet standard functions are not a large part (performance wise) of SPECfp 
programs, because that would be in conflict with the requirement that those 
programs "don't spend significant time in library routines" - a requirement 
that's not surprising if you take into account what SPECfp is trying to 
measure.

> It's remarkable that it's so poor---apparently the functions are so much
> worse than on x86 that it can negate all the advantage of a supposedly 2.5x
> SPECmark advantage. 

Well, one of the answers to this question might be that on the PII the 
transcendentals are translated (inline) to the equivalent machine 
instructions, while on Alpha they have to be library calls (Note: I didn't 
check this - so don't quote me on it).

This would at least forego the cost of making a subroutine call.

OTOH, if usage of exp, sin and cos is so heavy in your program, why don't you 
use lookup tables; you'll bet we (using a rotated lat-lon grid for limited 
area weather forecasting) do not want to invoke lots of sin and cos routines, 
whereas for the coordinate transformations between regular and rotated 
lat-lon, one *has* to.

